{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 9, 30]], "date-time": "2026-09-30T12:10:08Z", "timestamp": 1790770208175, "version": "4.1.0"}, "reference-count": 49, "publisher": "National Academy of Sciences", "issue": "38", "content-domain": {"domain": ["www.pnas.org"], "crossmark-restriction": true}, "short-container-title": ["Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A."], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2013, 9, 17]]}, "abstract": "<jats:title>Significance</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>The olfactory system of male moths exhibits the ability to detect minute amounts of sex pheromones. How this extreme sensitivity is achieved remains unclear. Using optogenetic techniques to activate a pheromone-responsive olfactory receptor neuron, our results reveal that weak olfactory inputs, but not strong inputs, are temporally integrated in second-order projection neurons to promote behavioral responsiveness. Furthermore, temporal integration of strong olfactory inputs is inhibited by GABAergic mechanisms, indicating that GABA signaling suppresses the amplification of strong stimuli. The timescale of this temporal integration corresponds well to the temporal dynamics of odor signals in the natural environment, suggesting that the olfactory systems of male moths use this mechanism to detect weak pheromone signals in the air.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1073/pnas.1313707110", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2013, 9, 5]], "date-time": "2013-09-05T00:47:33Z", "timestamp": 1378342053000}, "page": "15455-15460", "update-policy": "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.cm10313", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 52, "title": ["Pheromone responsiveness threshold depends on temporal integration by antennal lobe projection neurons"], "prefix": "10.1073", "volume": "110", "author": [{"given": "Masashi", "family": "Tabuchi", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Department of Advanced Interdisciplinary Studies, Graduate School of Engineering, and"}, {"name": "Research Ce